<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01374</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ddee1421-0b69-4ede-9041-0bd6ad4fe983</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="a5ee2468-69c0-4130-985e-a3f0c94a4142" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, \(w\) weeks after different chemicals were applied.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_01.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which algae bloom covered a larger area when the chemicals were applied?</para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve has a lower \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>Both algae blooms covered the same area after 5 weeks.</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para> Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because it has a higher \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: The algae bloom represented by the triangles because it has a higher \(y\)-intercept. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="08f2e010-a77c-447b-a9b7-796d79040bf5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The two graphs show models characterized by exponential decay representing the area covered by two different algae blooms, in square yards, \(w\) weeks after different chemicals were applied.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of two functions on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_02.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which algae population is decreasing more rapidly? Explain how you know.</para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve has a higher \(y\)-intercept.</para>
            <para>Both algae blooms are decreasing at the same rate.</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the circles because the curve is always below the other one on the graph.</para>
            <para>The algae bloom represented by the triangles because its graph is steeper.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curves graph is steeper. </para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is the algae bloom represented by the triangles because the curve's graph is steeper. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a89b1e97-30fc-428f-baba-62b0d7a9180b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_03.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>What fraction of the burn area remains each week?</para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(\frac12\) or \(.5\)</para>
            <para>\(\frac34\) or \(.75\)</para>
            <para>\(\frac14\) or \(.25\)</para>
            <para>\(\frac23\) or \(.66\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! .75 or \(\frac34\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.1. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is .75 or \(\frac34\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="dd028626-75d1-412e-80f9-2bb42d1333b5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_04.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>Which of the following is an equation representing the area of the burn, \(a\), after \(t\) weeks?</para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(a= 8 (\frac{1}{4})^t\)</para>
            <para>\(a= 8 (\frac{3}{4})^t\)</para>
            <para>\(a = -2t+8\)</para>
            <para>\(a= \frac{-6}{5}t+8\)  </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(a= 8 (\frac{3}{4})^t\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(a= 8 (\frac{3}{4})^t\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="816acdc7-11e3-4a82-8f9a-c533c67578ad" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>A medicine is applied to a burn on a patient’s arm. The area of the burn in square centimeters decreases exponentially and is shown in the graph.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Graph of a function on grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_05.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>What is the area of the burn after 7 weeks? Round to three decimal places.</para>
        </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 1.068</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 1.068. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="558b3142-705a-46fe-bd11-b132fffc0fc8" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The graphs show the amounts of medicine in two patients after receiving injections. The circles show the medicine in patient A, and the triangles show the medicine in patient B.
          
          <para><figure><media alt="Two functions plotted on the same grid."><image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_06.svg"/></media></figure></para>
          <para>One equation that gives the amount of medicine in milligrams, \(m\), in patient A, \(h\) hours after an injection, is \(m=300(\frac{1}{2})^h\).</para>
          <para>What could be an equation for the amount of medicine in patient B?</para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(m=500(\frac{3}{10})^h\)</para>
            <para>\(m=500(\frac{7}{10})^h\)</para>
            <para>\(m=200(\frac{3}{10})^h\)</para>
            <para>\( m=200(\frac{7}{10})^h\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( m=200(\frac{7}{10})^h\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( m=200(\frac{7}{10})^h\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="c0ce2bd6-b9f8-487b-8877-095fa40740a4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which scenario could describe the graph?

          <para><figure><media alt="GRAPH OF AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts OF 10 AND PASSING THROUGH THE POINTS (1, 20) AND (2, 40)."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_07.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
            <para>A savings account starts with $200 and decays by a factor of \(\frac12\) each month.</para>
            <para>A population of bacteria increases by a factor of 10 every hour and starts with 2 bacteria.</para>
            <para>A population of bacteria starts with 200 and decays by a factor of \(\frac12\) every hour.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: A population of bacteria doubles every hour and starts with 10 bacteria. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a1f8b0b4-7b47-4e6b-9bc9-229400a0148c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which equation could represent the graph?
          <para><figure><media alt="SCATTER PLOT THAT CAN BE MODELED BY AN INCREASING EXPONENTIAL FUNCTION WITH A \(y\)-intercepts THAT IS SLIGHTLY MORE THAN 2. THE SCATTER PLOT INCLUDES A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 1 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 3-POINT-1, A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 2 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 4-POINT-6, AND A POINT WITH AN X-VALUE OF 3 AND A Y-VALUE OF APPROXIMATELY 6-POINT-8."><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_09_10_08.jpg"/></media></figure></para>
        </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y= 2.104(1.479)^x\)</para>
            <para>\(y= 2.5x+2\)</para>
            <para>\(y= 2.104(.479)^x\)</para>
            <para>\(y=.479(2.104)^x\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.3.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y= 2.104(1.479)^x\). </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="1b655f00-f67d-49bb-9eca-e83983e2de0d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Given a formula for an exponential function, is it possible to determine whether the function grows or decays exponentially just by looking at the formula? Explain. </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>Yes, if the initial value is greater than one it’s growth; if the initial value is less than one it’s decay.</para>
            <para>Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one it is growth.</para>
            <para>Yes, if the growth rate is greater than one it’s growth; if the growth rate is, less than zero it’s decay.</para>
            <para>No, it is not possible to determine growth and decay from the formula.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.7.4. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is: Yes, if the growth rate is between zero and one, it’s decay; if the growth rate is greater than one, it’s growth. </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
  </content>
